# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: clal-sedit-recall-filename-quotes-and-tilde — recalled source path keeps its quotes and does not expand ~
#+STATUS: DONE
#+PRIORITY: P3
#+TYPE: bug
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: -

* Symptom

Found 2026-09-13 while investigating =navi-double-quit= (its trace shows it,
but it is a distinct defect from the quit semantics). Recalling a file-loaded
definition through =(CLAL-SEDIT 'FACT)= from the toplevel resolves the source
file to, literally:

: #P".../\"~/src/public/autolisp/fact.lsp\""

Two things are wrong with that one path:

1. The surrounding double-quote characters are KEPT inside the name — the
   recorded source string was =~/src/public/autolisp/fact.lsp= written with
   its reader quotes, and they were never stripped.
2. The leading =~= is NOT expanded to the home directory, and the whole
   (still-quoted) string is merged against the cwd, so it resolves nowhere.

* Cause (located, not yet fixed)

The symbol→recorded-file recall path:
- =%clal-sedit-target= resolves the symbol arg to the keyword under which the
  source file was recorded — =autolisp-builtins-core/source/api.lisp:5659=.
- The bad pathname surfaces in =source-file-open=, which uses
  =(merge-pathnames path)= — =autolisp-builtins-core/source/source-file.lisp:117-127=.
  =cl:merge-pathnames= does not expand =~=, and the incoming =path= still
  carries literal surrounding =\"= quote characters.

So the fix is on the recall path: strip the reader quotes from the recorded
source string and expand =~= (via =uiop:native-namestring= /
=uiop:parse-native-namestring=, or the project's existing home-expansion
helper) before handing it to =source-file-open=.

* Acceptance criteria

- =(clal-sedit 'fn)= for a function loaded from =~/…/fn.lsp= opens the real
  file (quotes stripped, =~= expanded), and a test covers the recall path.
- No change to opening a file given by an already-absolute/relative path.

* Note

Filed per the "write tickets for bugs found while doing something else" rule.
Surfaced in the =navi-double-quit= trace; see
=clautolisp/documentation/clautolisp-interactor-state-diagram.org= §3.

* Resolved — clautolisp 2.2.108

Fixed at the storage boundary rather than on one recall path, because a path
reaches sedit as TEXT from several directions and every one of them showed the
defect.

=CLAUTOLISP.SEDIT:NORMALIZE-SOURCE-PATH= (autolisp-sedit/source/session.lisp)
removes a matched pair of surrounding reader double quotes and expands a
leading =~= / =~/= through =USER-HOMEDIR-PATHNAME=; a pathname, NIL, an
absolute or relative path and a =~user= form pass through unchanged. It is
applied where a path becomes a pathname:

- =%RESOLVE-OPEN='s string branch — normalised ONCE, so the session ORIGIN
  records the corrected path and a later save writes the file the user meant;
- =%OPEN-FILE-NODE=;
- the editor's raw-argument commands =s= (=%DO-SAVE=), =w= (=%DO-WRITE-NEW=)
  and =load= (=%DO-LOAD=) in modes.lisp — the tokens an interactor read, which
  is where the quotes come from in the first place;
- =SOURCE-FILE-OPEN= and =WRITE-NEW-SOURCE-FILE= in
  autolisp-builtins-core/source/source-file.lisp (the site named in /Cause/).

Measured, not assumed: both host Lisps mishandle a quoted path, and they
disagree about =~=. For a quoted =~/x.lsp= SBCL's MERGE-PATHNAMES yields
=<cwd>/"~/x.lsp"= (pjb's symptom exactly) and CCL ="~/x.lsp"= with the quotes
IN the name; an unquoted =~/x.lsp= keeps its =~/= under SBCL but is expanded at
parse time under CCL, and a bare =~= becomes a file NAMED =~= under SBCL. So
the expansion is also what makes a recorded path host-independent.

Tests: =normalize-source-path-strips-reader-quotes-and-expands-tilde=,
=open-a-quoted-path-opens-the-real-file= and
=open-a-tilde-path-opens-the-file-under-the-home-directory=
(autolisp-sedit/tests/session-tests.lisp) plus
=source-file-open-accepts-a-path-as-the-user-typed-it=
(autolisp-builtins-core/tests/source-file-tests.lisp). Verified on the BUILT
binary too: a quoted =~/…/fact.lsp= handed to =clal-sedit= printed =[]= — an
empty editor — before, and opens the real =defun= now.

The acceptance criterion "no change to opening a file given by an
already-absolute/relative path" is covered by the unchanged-input assertions
and by the pre-existing file/directory open tests, which still pass.

Documented in clautolisp-user-manual.org § /Starting sedit from AutoLISP/
("How a path may be spelled") and in RELEASE_NOTES.org.
